.TH DOME-COMPOSITOR 1 "2026-10-21" "de-xr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dome\-compositor \- Wayland desktop and XR session compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
dome\-compositor [\-\-backend drm|nested] [\-\-renderer vulkan|gles]
                [\-\-terminal | \-\-command PROGRAM ARGS...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
DomeD manages a conventional desktop and a shared spatial desktop. Its native DRM backend owns the seat and displays; the nested backend opens a development window in an existing desktop. The dome\-xr process presents live client textures through OpenXR.
.SH TOOLKIT
The default native shell uses patched FLTK 1.4.5 with Cairo/FreeType rendering.
Qt6 is a build-time alternative selected with Cargo --no-default-features and
--features nested,standalone,xr,qt6. It is not a runtime switch.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-\-backend drm|nested"
Select native DRM/KMS or nested development mode. Without an explicit choice, an existing DISPLAY or WAYLAND_DISPLAY selects nested mode; otherwise DRM is selected.
.TP
.B "\-\-renderer vulkan|gles"
Select Vulkan through Mesa Zink (default), or GLES.
.TP
.B "\-\-check\-backend"
Report compiled backend support without opening a seat. Combine with \-\-backend.
.TP
.B "\-\-refresh\-session\-fonts"
Update the stable font export in XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dome-fonts and refresh older DomeD font export paths for launchers which outlived a compositor upgrade. No logout or display connection is required.
.TP
.B "\-\-export\-fonts DIRECTORY"
Export the shell's complete bitmap font bundle and a fonts.conf file without opening a display or starting a session. Use the parent directory of the session's FONTCONFIG_FILE to refresh its fonts after upgrading DomeD. Existing themes and the selected font remain unchanged.
.TP
.B "\-\-terminal"
Launch the configured terminal initially.
.TP
.B "\-\-command PROGRAM ARGS..."
Launch a custom client. Remaining arguments belong to that client.
.TP
.B "\-\-help"
Show usage.
.SH WINDOW TRANSFER
Super+Shift+X, or Window > Send Window to XR / Send Window to Desktop, transfers the focused
Wayland or X11 window and its dialogs between DomeD's views without restarting
the application. The window appears exclusively at its destination; other windows
of the application stay in place. Both desktop geometry and XR placement are
retained. Window > Show Window on Both restores simultaneous desktop and XR
presentation, including dialogs. The three explicit choices show the current
presentation with a radio marker. Focus stays in the source view after an exclusive transfer. Switch input with the taskbar's
Desktop / XR button to use the destination. Sending to XR requires an active
XR session. If XR stops, its windows return to the desktop. The shortcut can be
changed in keybindings. Native OpenXR overlays and other compositors are not
supported by this action.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
DOME_BACKEND and DOME_RENDERER select backend and renderer. DOME_CONFIG selects a geometry profile. DOME_PREVIEW_PORT selects the loopback bridge port (default 49640). DOME_TERMINAL selects a terminal executable without arguments.
.PP
Native DRM uses one GPU unless compiled with the Cargo feature multi-gpu.
DOME_BUILD_DRM_DEVICE is a build-time absolute device path that pins a single-GPU
binary, preferably using /dev/dri/by-path. Otherwise DOME_DRM_DEVICE selects one
card at runtime, defaulting to the seat's primary GPU in single-GPU builds.
A build-time pin takes precedence over DOME_DRM_DEVICE. Multi-GPU support cannot
be enabled at runtime. Use --check-backend to inspect these build settings.
.SH INTERACTION
XR uses cylindrical dome windows. Super+P pulls the focused window in front of the user or returns it to the dome; at most two windows can be pulled out. The default distance is 2 metres. Settings configure distance, dome height and bindings. Mouse and keyboard are the only input methods. Alt+Tab and Alt+Shift+Tab switch windows in the same lane.
.PP
Super+Alt+Up/Down moves the focused window between lanes. Super+Alt+Left/Right reorders it horizontally within its lane, stopping at either edge. Moving a pulled-out window returns it to the dome. Super+Equals/Minus multiplies/divides the focused XR window size by 1.1, within 0.25\(en4 times scale. The View menu exposes lane and size actions; Settings allows rebinding.
.PP
Super+W closes the focused window. Ctrl+W is left to applications for closing tabs;
Super+Q closes the active application. Alt+F4 is not a default window-close shortcut.
Saved stock Alt+F4 bindings migrate to Super+W; custom remaps and disabled actions are preserved.
.SH UNIVERSAL MENUS
File, Edit and Window are always present, including for Electron, Vivaldi and other
applications without exported menus. File and Edit follow the application menu; Window
precedes an exported Help menu. File supplies Close Window. Edit supplies Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Paste and
Select All when those operations are missing from the export. Fallback operations
forward the usual Ctrl shortcuts to the focused client, which decides whether
the current selection or document supports them. The application
menu supplies About, Hide and Quit. Exported File, Edit and Window menus keep their
native actions and gain compositor controls separated from those actions.
Without an exporter, File, Edit, View, Window and Help use compositor actions.
Window offers Send Window to Desktop, Send Window to XR and Show Window on Both.
Unfocused window actions are disabled; XR destinations require an active session.
.PP
Application-specific menus require KDE AppMenu/DBusMenu or GTK menu exports.
Chromium/Electron support depends on the bundled version and backend. Private
HTML menus and menus disabled by the application cannot be reconstructed.
.SH MENU NAVIGATION
Super+F10 opens the universal menu. Left/Right switch enabled headers.
Up/Down wrap through enabled rows, skipping separators. Right enters a
submenu; Left returns to its parent and restores the selected parent row.
Home/End select the first/last enabled row. Enter, keypad Enter and Space
activate or open it. Escape dismisses the menu. Moving the pointer resumes
mouse selection; a stationary XR mouse ray does not replace keyboard selection.
.PP
Inactive desktops containing XR windows remain visible as deeper, faded dome
layers. Only the active desktop accepts input; switching desktops brings the
selected desktop to the front. Minimized and desktop-only windows are omitted.
.PP
The Desktops switcher shows each desktop's open-window count, including minimized
windows and windows presented only in XR, or Empty when none are open.
Click Rename beside a desktop, or select it with the arrow keys and press F2.
Enter or Save applies the name; Escape or Cancel discards the edit.
Names support 1\(en32 printable Unicode characters and are saved in the session
layout. Renaming leaves the active desktop and window placement unchanged.
The rename buttons also accept the XR bottom-panel pointer.
.SH CURSOR
DomeD owns cursor appearance and visibility globally. Applications cannot replace
the cursor image or hotspot, or hide it. Native and nested backends draw the
DomeD arrow; the nested host cursor is hidden to avoid duplicate pointers.
Appearance changes take effect immediately: dark mode uses a light cursor and
light mode uses a dark cursor, each with a contrasting outline. The XR reticle
uses the same palette. Cursor visibility during desktop/XR input handover remains
under compositor control.
.SH CURSOR FINDER
Double-click the middle mouse button within 400 milliseconds, with at most
eight logical pixels of desktop pointer movement (a nearby ray position in XR),
to show three short circular pulses
contracting towards the live cursor hotspot. The accent-coloured effect lasts
660 milliseconds on desktop and XR. Both normal middle-button press/release
pairs continue to the target. Changing input realms cancels the gesture.
.SH FOCUS FEEDBACK
The focused window receives a subtle accent-coloured one-pixel inner border
and a faint second pixel. It also applies to client-decorated and maximized
windows. The effect follows the Appearance accent without changing window
geometry or input regions. XR stereo windows receive an edge in each eye.
.SH BITMAP FONTS
The font picker offers native BDF strikes with separate family, face, size and
integer scale controls. The distribution includes GNU Unifont, Unifont Upper,
Galmuri, Fusion Pixel, Ark Pixel, Jelly Pixel, Retro Pixel, MaruMinya,
MaruMinyaHangul and the curated hicc pixel fonts. Kirsch and Kissinger2 are
retired. Powerline and emoji coverage remains face-dependent; the retired
Kirsch private-use Nerd Font glyphs are no longer bundled.
.PP
Fusion Pixel, Ark Pixel and Jelly Pixel combine their regional source strikes
into one mixed-script BDF per size and spacing mode. Unicode 17 Script selects
Kana from Japanese, Hangul from Korean and Bopomofo from Taiwanese sources.
Unified Han code points cannot identify regional forms; they use the
Simplified Chinese source with a stable missing-glyph fallback order.
Existing Fusion Pixel language selections migrate to the combined strike.
There is no separate script control.
.PP
Font licenses and provenance are installed under
/usr/local/share/doc/domed/fonts.
.SH GMSH STEREO VIEW
The FLTK Gmsh client uses org.gmsh.Sbs for its live mesh-only side-by-side
window. DomeD initially presents this window only in XR and crops the left and
right texture halves for the corresponding eyes. Gmsh retains its desktop GUI
and uses DomeD's existing XR session and native GPU transport.
.PP
Start XR, then select Window / DomeD XR Mesh in Gmsh, press Ctrl+Shift+X, or run
gmsh-dome-xr mesh.msh inside the DomeD Wayland session. The version-1
/api/integrations/gmsh endpoint advertises session identity and availability.
.SH SEE ALSO
dome\-xr(1), dome\-preview(1), ghostty\-xr(1)
